<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    AI cross-level jumping (§83b).

    Hung off vanilla's OWN modder insertion hook rather than patched into the Humanlike tree
    by XPath: ThinkNode_SubtreesByTag splices every ThinkTreeDef declaring the matching
    insertTag, so this adds a node without touching a vanilla def and cannot collide with
    another mod doing the same thing.

    WHICH TAG IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THE FIRST CHOICE WAS WRONG (run #384).

    Humanlike_PostDuty looked perfect: a pawn reaches it only once the Lord duty subtree has
    produced no job, which reads as "the AI wanted to get somewhere and could not" for free.
    It is not what that tag means. A duty that fails to reach anything does NOT return empty -
    AssaultColony falls back to a wander/wait job, so the duty node returns a Job, the priority
    root stops there, and the tag is never evaluated. The probe caught it exactly: a raider
    with a perfect leap solution sat at `curJob=Wait_Wander, duty=AssaultColony` and the node
    had never once been asked.

    Humanlike_PostMentalState is evaluated on every think, ahead of the Lord duty, and it is
    the neighbourhood vanilla itself uses for ability reflexes - Humanlike.xml line 66 puts
    `Abilities_Escape` two entries above this tag. Preempting the duty is safe here because the
    giver's own gate is that the pawn CANNOT WALK to its goal: if walking works, the duty is
    left alone.

    (`Abilities_Aggressive` was the other candidate. Rejected: it is referenced from DutyDefs
    rather than inserted by tag, so using it would mean patching vanilla defs, and its
    ThinkNode_ConditionalColonist/invert gate would exclude colonists - which contradicts the
    user's "anyone with a jump pack or jump ability".)
  -->
  <ThinkTreeDef>
    <defName>AB2_BandLeap</defName>
    <insertTag>Humanlike_PostMentalState</insertTag>
    <insertPriority>50</insertPriority>
    <thinkRoot Class="ThinkNode_Priority">
      <subNodes>
        <li Class="AsAboveSoBelow.JobGiver_ABBandLeap" />
      </subNodes>
    </thinkRoot>
  </ThinkTreeDef>

</Defs>
